"[2602.13303v1] Spectral Collapse in Diffusion Inversion (https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.13303v1)\nciteturn31886view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.13303v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 163\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 9 Feb 2026]\nL9: # Title:Spectral Collapse in Diffusion Inversion\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Nicolas Bourriez , cite6†Alexandre Verine , cite7†Auguste Genovesio L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Spectral Collapse in Diffusion Inversion, by Nicolas Bourriez and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite8†View PDF cite9†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Conditional diffusion inversion provides a powerful framework for unpaired image-to-image translation. However, we demonstrate through an extensive analysis that standard deterministic inversion (e.g. DDIM) fails when the source domain is spectrally sparse compared to the target domain (e.g., super-resolution, sketch-to-image). In these contexts, the recovered latent from the input does not follow the expected isotropic Gaussian distribution.\nL17: Instead it exhibits a signal with lower frequencies, locking target sampling to oversmoothed and texture-poor generations. We term this phenomenon spectral collapse. We observe that stochastic alternatives attempting to restore the noise variance tend to break the semantic link to the input, leading to structural drift.\nL18: To resolve this structure-texture trade-off, we propose Orthogonal Variance Guidance (OVG), an inference-time method that corrects the ODE dynamics to enforce the theoretical Gaussian noise magnitude within the null-space of the structural gradient. Extensive experiments on microscopy super-resolution (BBBC021) and sketch-to-image (Edges2Shoes) demonstrate that OVG effectively restores photorealistic textures while preserving structural fidelity.\nL19: Subjects:  | Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG); Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite10†arXiv:2602.13303 [cs.CV]\nL21:    | (or cite11†arXiv:2602.13303v1 [cs.CV] for this version)\nL22:    | cite12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.13303†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Nicolas Bourriez [cite13†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Mon, 9 Feb 2026 17:53:21 UTC (28,294 KB)\nL27: \nL28: Full-text links:\nL29: \nL30: ## Access Paper:\nL31: \nL32: View a PDF of the paper titled Spectral Collapse in Diffusion Inversion, by Nicolas Bourriez and 2 other authors\nL33: \nL34:   * cite8†View PDF L35:   * cite9†HTML (experimental) L36:   * cite14†TeX Source L37: \nL38: cite15†view license†creativecommons.org L39: ### Current browse context:\nL40: \nL41: cs.CV\nL42: \nL43: cite16†< prev |   cite17†next > L44: \nL45: cite18†new | cite19†recent | cite20†2026-02 L46: \nL47: Change to browse by:\nL48: \nL49: cite21†cs L50: cite22†cs.AI L51: cite23†cs.LG L52: cite24†eess L53: cite25†eess.IV L54: \nL55: ### References & Citations\nL56: \nL57:   * cite26†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L58:   * cite27†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L59:   * cite28†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L60: \nL61: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL62: \nL63: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL64: \nL65: [Button: ×]\nL66: \nL67: loading...\nL68: \nL69: Data provided by:\nL70: \nL71: ### Bookmark\nL72: \nL73: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL74: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL75: \nL76: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL77: \nL78: Bibliographic Explorer (cite29†What